answer:
Verdict: bug found

Line 12: `if (ready==0) pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m);`

The if guard tests once. An allowed spurious return permits the assertion or later consumption even when ready is still zero.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | lock(m) succeeds |  | m=A, ready=0 |
| 2 | read ready -> 0 |  | m=A |
| 3 | cond_wait: release-and-wait |  | A waiting; B not yet scheduled |
| 4 | cond_wait returns spuriously, reacquiring m |  | m=A, ready=0 |
| 5 | assert ready equals expected fails |  | no predicate recheck; ready=0 |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 static int ready;
 static void *consumer(void *arg) {
     (void)arg; pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
-    if (ready==0) pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m);
+    while (ready==0) pthread_cond_wait(&cv,&m);
     assert(ready==4); pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); return NULL;
 }
 static void *producer(void *arg) {
```

The while guard re-evaluates ready after every return with m held. A false predicate goes back to waiting, including after spurious or competing-consumer wakeups.

question:
Is this 4-unit update's mutex entry section correct, or can I unlock first to make sure m is free? Assume successful API calls unless the ownership protocol itself is invalid.
```c
#define _XOPEN_SOURCE 700
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <assert.h>
#include <stdio.h>
static pthread_mutex_t m=PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static int count;
static void *worker(void *arg) {
