question:
Why does this normal-mutex program never finish its intended count += 7? Assume successful API calls unless the ownership protocol itself is invalid.
```c
#define _XOPEN_SOURCE 700
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <assert.h>
#include <stdio.h>
static pthread_mutex_t m;
static int count;
static void *worker(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    count += 7;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_mutexattr_t attr; pthread_t a;
    pthread_mutexattr_init(&attr); pthread_mutexattr_settype(&attr,PTHREAD_MUTEX_NORMAL);
    pthread_mutex_init(&m,&attr); pthread_mutexattr_destroy(&attr);
    pthread_create(&a,NULL,worker,NULL); pthread_join(a,NULL);
    printf("%d\n",count); pthread_mutex_destroy(&m); return 0;
}
```

answer:
Verdict: bug found

Line 12: `pthread_mutex_lock(&m);`

The second consecutive lock is the faulty acquisition. m is explicitly PTHREAD_MUTEX_NORMAL, so self-relocking waits on ownership that this same worker must release.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | first lock(m) succeeds |  | m=A |
| 2 | second lock(m) blocks |  | A waits for its own normal mutex |
| 3 |  | main waits in join(A) | A cannot reach its exit section |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -9,7 +9,6 @@
 static void *worker(void *arg) {
     (void)arg;
     pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
-    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
     count += 7;
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); return NULL;
 }
```

One lock surrounds the update, then one unlock releases it. Removing the duplicate avoids self-deadlock without hiding the problem behind a recursive mutex.

question:
Is this program guaranteed to print the completed result 7, and is its shared read synchronized? Assume successful API calls unless the ownership protocol itself is invalid.
