Line 13: `pthread_t a,b; sem_init(&gate,0,0);`

The gate starts with zero permits although the critical section should initially be available. No thread can reach the post that would create a permit.

| Step | Thread A | Thread B | Shared state |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | sem_wait(gate) blocks |  | gate=0; no owner |
| 2 |  | sem_wait(gate) blocks | gate=0; every sem_post lies after a blocked wait |

Fix:
```diff
--- prog.c
+++ prog.c (fixed)
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@
     (void)arg; sem_wait(&gate); count += 13; sem_post(&gate); return NULL;
 }
 int main(void) {
-    pthread_t a,b; sem_init(&gate,0,0);
+    pthread_t a,b; sem_init(&gate,0,1);
     pthread_create(&a,NULL,worker,NULL); pthread_create(&b,NULL,worker,NULL);
     pthread_join(a,NULL); pthread_join(b,NULL);
     printf("%d\n",count); sem_destroy(&gate); return 0;
```

Initialize the mutex-like semaphore to one permit. Balanced wait/signal (P/V), sem_wait/sem_post, serializes the updates without inventing extra permits.

